#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Kursus IBM percuma tentang maklumat dan pengkomputeran kuantum"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Toric code"
msgstr "Kod torik"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"{/* cspell:ignore LDPC */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore tensoring */}\n"
msgstr ""
"{/* cspell:ignore LDPC */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore tensoring */}\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next we'll discuss a specific CSS code known as the *toric code*, which was discovered by Alexei Kitaev in 1997.\n"
"In fact, the toric code isn't a single code, but rather it's a family of codes, one for each positive integer starting from 2.\n"
"These codes possess a few key properties:\n"
msgstr ""
"Seterusnya kita akan membincangkan kod CSS tertentu yang dikenali sebagai *kod torik*, yang ditemui oleh Alexei Kitaev pada tahun 1997.\n"
"Sebenarnya, kod torik bukan satu kod tunggal, tetapi merupakan satu keluarga kod, satu untuk setiap integer positif bermula dari 2.\n"
"Kod-kod ini mempunyai beberapa sifat utama:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The stabilizer generators have *low weight*, and in particular they all have weight 4. In coding theory parlance, the toric code is an example of a quantum low-density parity check code, or *quantum LDPC code* (where *low* means 4 in this case). This is nice because each stabilizer generator measurement doesn't need to involve too many qubits.\n"
msgstr "Penjana penstabilan mempunyai *berat rendah*, dan khususnya semuanya mempunyai berat 4. Dalam istilah teori pengekodan, kod torik adalah contoh kod semak pariti ketumpatan rendah kuantum, atau *kod LDPC kuantum* (di mana *rendah* bermaksud 4 dalam kes ini). Ini bagus kerana setiap pengukuran penjana penstabilan tidak perlu melibatkan terlalu banyak qubit.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The toric code has *geometric locality*. This means that not only do the stabilizer generators have low weight, but it's also possible to arrange the qubits spatially so that each of the stabilizer generator measurements only involves qubits that are close together. In principle, this makes these measurements easier to implement than if they involved spatially distant qubits.\n"
msgstr "Kod torik mempunyai *lokaliti geometri*. Ini bermakna bukan sahaja penjana penstabilan mempunyai berat rendah, tetapi juga boleh menyusun qubit secara ruang supaya setiap pengukuran penjana penstabilan hanya melibatkan qubit yang berdekatan. Pada dasarnya, ini menjadikan pengukuran ini lebih mudah dilaksanakan berbanding jika ia melibatkan qubit yang berjauhan secara ruang.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Members of the toric code family have increasingly *large distance* and can tolerate a relatively *high error rate*.\n"
msgstr "Ahli keluarga kod torik mempunyai *jarak yang semakin besar* dan boleh menoleransi *kadar ralat yang agak tinggi*.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Toric code description {#toric-code-description}"
msgstr "Huraian kod torik {#toric-code-description}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let $L\\geq 2$ be a positive integer, and consider an $L\\times L$ lattice with so-called *periodic boundaries*.\n"
"For example, this figure depicts an $L\\times L$ lattice for $L=9$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Biar $L\\geq 2$ menjadi integer positif, dan pertimbangkan kekisi $L\\times L$ dengan apa yang dipanggil *sempadan berkala*.\n"
"Sebagai contoh, rajah ini menggambarkan kekisi $L\\times L$ untuk $L=9$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A 9-by-9 lattice with periodic boundaries](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/9-by-9-lattice.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Kekisi 9-kali-9 dengan sempadan berkala](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/9-by-9-lattice.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Notice that the lines on the right and on the bottom are dotted lines.\n"
"This is meant to indicate that dotted line on the right is the *same line* as the line all the way on the left, and similarly, the dotted line on the bottom is the same line as the one on the very top.\n"
msgstr ""
"Perhatikan bahawa garisan di sebelah kanan dan di bawah adalah garisan bertitik.\n"
"Ini bertujuan untuk menunjukkan bahawa garisan bertitik di sebelah kanan adalah *garisan yang sama* dengan garisan di sebelah kiri, dan begitu juga, garisan bertitik di bawah adalah garisan yang sama dengan garisan di bahagian paling atas.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To realize this sort of configuration physically requires three dimensions.\n"
"In particular, we could form the lattice into a cylinder by first matching up the left and right sides, and then bend the cylinder around so that the circles at the ends, which used to be the top and bottom edges of the lattice, meet.\n"
"Or we could match up the top and bottom first and then the sides; it works both ways and doesn't matter which we choose for the purposes of this discussion.\n"
msgstr ""
"Untuk merealisasikan konfigurasi seperti ini secara fizikal memerlukan tiga dimensi.\n"
"Khususnya, kita boleh membentuk kekisi itu menjadi silinder dengan terlebih dahulu menyamakan sisi kiri dan kanan, kemudian membengkokkan silinder itu supaya bulatan di hujungnya, yang dahulunya adalah tepi atas dan bawah kekisi, bertemu.\n"
"Atau kita boleh menyamakan bahagian atas dan bawah terlebih dahulu kemudian bahagian sisinya; kedua-duanya berfungsi dan tidak kira mana yang kita pilih untuk tujuan perbincangan ini.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "What we obtain is a *torus* — or, in other words, a doughnut (although thinking about it as an inner tube of a tire is perhaps a better image to have in mind because this isn't a solid: the lattice has become just the *surface* of a torus). This is where the name \"toric code\" comes from.\n"
msgstr "Apa yang kita perolehi ialah *torus* — atau dengan kata lain, donat (walaupun memikirkannya sebagai tiub dalam tayar mungkin gambaran yang lebih baik kerana ini bukan pepejal: kekisi itu telah menjadi hanya *permukaan* torus). Inilah asal-usul nama \"kod torik\".\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A 9-by-9 lattice wrapped into a torus](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/torus.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Kekisi 9-kali-9 yang dililitkan menjadi torus](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/torus.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The way one can \"move around\" on a torus like this, between adjacent points on the lattice, will likely be familiar to those that have played old-school video games, where moving off the top of the screen causes you emerge on the bottom, and likewise for the left and right edges of the screen.\n"
"This is how we will view this lattice with periodic boundaries, as opposed to speaking specifically about a torus in 3-dimensional space.\n"
msgstr ""
"Cara seseorang boleh \"bergerak\" pada torus seperti ini, antara titik bersebelahan pada kekisi, mungkin sudah biasa bagi mereka yang pernah bermain permainan video lama, di mana bergerak keluar dari bahagian atas skrin menyebabkan anda muncul di bawah, dan begitu juga untuk tepi kiri dan kanan skrin.\n"
"Beginilah cara kita akan melihat kekisi dengan sempadan berkala ini, berbanding dengan bercakap secara khusus tentang torus dalam ruang 3-dimensi.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, qubits are arranged on the *edges* of this lattice, as illustrated in the following figure, where qubits are indicated by solid blue circles.\n"
msgstr "Seterusnya, qubit disusun pada *tepi* kekisi ini, seperti yang digambarkan dalam rajah berikut, di mana qubit ditunjukkan dengan bulatan biru pepejal.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Qubits on the edges of a 9-by-9 periodic lattice](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/qubits-on-lattice-edges.svg)\n"
msgstr "![qubit pada tepi kekisi berkala 9-kali-9](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/qubits-on-lattice-edges.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that the qubits placed on the dotted lines aren't solid because they're already represented on the topmost and leftmost lines in the lattice.\n"
"In total there are $2L^2$ qubits: $L^2$ qubits on horizontal lines and $L^2$ qubits on vertical lines.\n"
msgstr ""
"Perhatikan bahawa qubit yang diletakkan pada garisan bertitik tidak pepejal kerana ia sudah diwakili pada garisan paling atas dan paling kiri dalam kekisi.\n"
"Secara keseluruhan terdapat $2L^2$ qubit: $L^2$ qubit pada garisan mendatar dan $L^2$ qubit pada garisan menegak.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To describe the toric code itself, it remains to describe the stabilizer generators:\n"
msgstr "Untuk menghuraikan kod torik itu sendiri, yang tinggal adalah menghuraikan penjana penstabilan:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "For each *tile* formed by the lines in the lattice there is one $Z$ stabilizer generator, obtained by tensoring $Z$ matrices on the four qubits touching that tile along with identity matrices on all other qubits.\n"
msgstr "Untuk setiap *jubin* yang dibentuk oleh garisan dalam kekisi, terdapat satu penjana penstabilan $Z$, yang diperoleh dengan mentensori matriks $Z$ pada empat qubit yang menyentuh jubin itu bersama matriks identiti pada semua qubit yang lain.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "For each *vertex* formed by the lines in the lattice there is one $X$ stabilizer generator, obtained by tensoring $X$ matrices on the four qubits adjacent to that vertex along with identity matrices on all other qubits.\n"
msgstr "Untuk setiap *verteks* yang dibentuk oleh garisan dalam kekisi, terdapat satu penjana penstabilan $X$, yang diperoleh dengan mentensori matriks $X$ pada empat qubit bersebelahan dengan verteks itu bersama matriks identiti pada semua qubit yang lain.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In both cases we obtain a weight-4 Pauli operation. Individually, these stabilizer generators may be illustrated as follows.\n"
msgstr "Dalam kedua-dua kes, kita mendapat operasi Pauli berat-4. Secara individu, penjana penstabilan ini boleh digambarkan seperti berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Stabilizer generator types for the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Jenis penjana penstabilan untuk kod torik](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here's an illustration showing some examples of stabilizer generators in the context of the lattice itself.\n"
"Notice that the stabilizer generators that wrap around the periodic boundaries are included.\n"
"These generators that wrap around the periodic boundaries are not special or in any way distinguished from the ones that don't.\n"
msgstr ""
"Berikut adalah ilustrasi yang menunjukkan beberapa contoh penjana penstabilan dalam konteks kekisi itu sendiri.\n"
"Perhatikan bahawa penjana penstabilan yang melintasi sempadan berkala turut disertakan.\n"
"Penjana yang melintasi sempadan berkala ini tidak istimewa atau berbeza dalam apa jua cara daripada yang tidak melintasinya.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Examples of stabilizer generators on a lattice](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/stabilizer-generator-examples.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Contoh penjana penstabilan pada kekisi](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/stabilizer-generator-examples.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The stabilizer generators must commute for this to be a valid stabilizer code.\n"
"As usual, the $Z$ stabilizer generators all commute with one another, because $Z$ commutes with itself and the identity commutes with everything, and likewise for the $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
"The $Z$ and $X$ stabilizer generators clearly commute when they act nontrivially on disjoint sets of qubits, like for the examples shown in the previous figure.\n"
"The remaining possibility is that a $Z$ stabilizer generator and an $X$ stabilizer generator overlap on the qubits upon which they act nontrivially, and whenever this happens the generators must always overlap on two qubits, like in the next figure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Penjana penstabilan mestilah kalis tukar tertib (komut) untuk ini menjadi kod penstabilan yang sah.\n"
"Seperti biasa, semua penjana penstabilan $Z$ kalis tukar tertib antara satu sama lain, kerana $Z$ kalis tukar tertib dengan dirinya sendiri dan identiti kalis tukar tertib dengan segalanya, dan begitu juga untuk penjana penstabilan $X$.\n"
"Penjana penstabilan $Z$ dan $X$ jelas kalis tukar tertib apabila ia bertindak secara bukan remeh pada set qubit yang tidak bertindih, seperti dalam contoh yang ditunjukkan dalam rajah sebelumnya.\n"
"Kemungkinan yang tinggal ialah penjana penstabilan $Z$ dan penjana penstabilan $X$ bertindih pada qubit di mana ia bertindak secara bukan remeh, dan apabila ini berlaku, penjana-penjana itu sentiasa bertindih pada dua qubit, seperti dalam rajah berikutnya.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Overlapping stabilizer generators for the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/stabilizer-generators-overlap.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Penjana penstabilan yang bertindih untuk kod torik](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/stabilizer-generators-overlap.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consequently, two stabilizer generators like this commute, just like $Z\\otimes Z$ and $X\\otimes X$ commute.\n"
"The stabilizer generators therefore all commute with one another.\n"
msgstr ""
"Akibatnya, dua penjana penstabilan seperti ini kalis tukar tertib, sama seperti $Z\\otimes Z$ dan $X\\otimes X$ kalis tukar tertib.\n"
"Oleh itu, semua penjana penstabilan kalis tukar tertib antara satu sama lain.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second required condition on the stabilizer generators for a stabilizer code is that they form a minimal generating set.\n"
"This condition is actually *not* satisfied by this collection: if we multiply all of the $Z$ stabilizer generators together, we obtain the identity operation, and likewise for the $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
"Thus, any one of the $Z$ stabilizer generators can be expressed as the product of all of the remaining ones, and similarly, any one of the $X$ stabilizer generators can be expressed as the product of the remaining $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
"If we remove any one of the $Z$ stabilizer generators and any one of the $X$ stabilizer generators, however, we do obtain a minimal generating set.\n"
msgstr ""
"Syarat kedua yang diperlukan pada penjana penstabilan untuk kod penstabilan ialah ia mestilah membentuk set penjanaan minimum.\n"
"Syarat ini sebenarnya *tidak* dipenuhi oleh koleksi ini: jika kita darabkan semua penjana penstabilan $Z$ bersama-sama, kita mendapat operasi identiti, dan begitu juga untuk penjana penstabilan $X$.\n"
"Oleh itu, mana-mana satu penjana penstabilan $Z$ boleh dinyatakan sebagai hasil darab semua yang lain, dan begitu juga, mana-mana satu penjana penstabilan $X$ boleh dinyatakan sebagai hasil darab penjana penstabilan $X$ yang selebihnya.\n"
"Namun, jika kita membuang mana-mana satu penjana penstabilan $Z$ dan mana-mana satu penjana penstabilan $X$, kita memang mendapat set penjanaan minimum.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be clear about this, we do in fact care equally about all of the stabilizer generators, and in a strictly operational sense there isn't any need to select one stabilizer generator of each type to remove.\n"
"But, for the sake of *analyzing* the code — and counting the generators in particular — we can imagine that one stabilizer generator of each type has been removed, so that we get a minimal generating set, keeping in mind that we could always infer the results of these removed generators (thinking of them as observables) from the results of all of the other stabilizer generator observables of the same type.\n"
msgstr ""
"Untuk jelaskan perkara ini, kita memang mengambil berat tentang semua penjana penstabilan secara sama rata, dan secara operasi yang ketat tidak ada keperluan untuk memilih satu penjana penstabilan setiap jenis untuk dibuang.\n"
"Namun, demi *menganalisis* kod — dan mengira penjana khususnya — kita boleh bayangkan bahawa satu penjana penstabilan setiap jenis telah dibuang, supaya kita mendapat set penjanaan minimum, sambil ingat bahawa kita sentiasa boleh menyimpulkan keputusan penjana yang dibuang ini (dengan menganggapnya sebagai boleh cerapan) daripada keputusan semua boleh cerapan penjana penstabilan lain yang berjenis sama.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This leaves $L^2 - 1$ stabilizer generators of each type, or $2L^2 - 2$ in total, in a (hypothetical) minimal generating set.\n"
"Given that there are $2L^2$ qubits in total, this means that the toric code encodes $2L^2 - 2 (L^2 - 1) = 2$ qubits.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ini meninggalkan $L^2 - 1$ penjana penstabilan setiap jenis, atau $2L^2 - 2$ secara keseluruhan, dalam set penjanaan minimum (hipotetikal).\n"
"Memandangkan terdapat $2L^2$ qubit secara keseluruhan, ini bermakna kod torik mengekod $2L^2 - 2 (L^2 - 1) = 2$ qubit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The final condition required of stabilizer generators is that at least one quantum state vector is fixed by all of the stabilizer generators.\n"
"We will see that this is the case as we proceed with the analysis of the code, but it's also possible to reason that there's no way to generate $-1$ times the identity on all $2L^2$ qubits from the stabilizer generators.\n"
msgstr ""
"Syarat terakhir yang diperlukan pada penjana penstabilan ialah sekurang-kurangnya satu vektor keadaan kuantum ditetapkan oleh semua penjana penstabilan.\n"
"Kita akan melihat bahawa ini memang berlaku apabila kita meneruskan analisis kod, tetapi juga boleh difikirkan bahawa tiada cara untuk menjana $-1$ kali identiti pada semua $2L^2$ qubit daripada penjana penstabilan.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Detecting errors {#detecting-errors}"
msgstr "Mengesan ralat {#detecting-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The toric code has a simple and elegant description, but its quantum error-correcting properties may not be at all clear from a first glance.\n"
"As it turns out, it's an amazing code!\n"
"To understand why and how it works, let's begin by considering different errors and the syndromes they generate.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kod torik mempunyai huraian yang mudah dan elegan, tetapi sifat pembetulan ralat kuantumnya mungkin tidak jelas pada pandangan pertama.\n"
"Ternyata, ia adalah kod yang menakjubkan!\n"
"Untuk memahami mengapa dan bagaimana ia berfungsi, mari kita mulakan dengan mempertimbangkan ralat yang berbeza dan sindrom yang dihasilkannya.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The toric code is a CSS code, because all of our stabilizer generators are either $Z$ or $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
"This means that $X$ errors and $Z$ errors can be detected (and possibly corrected) separately.\n"
"In fact, there's a simple symmetry between the $Z$ and $X$ stabilizer generators that allows us to analyze $X$ errors and $Z$ errors in essentially the same way.\n"
"So, we shall focus on $X$ errors, which are possibly detected by the $Z$ stabilizer generators — but the entire discussion that follows can be translated from $X$ errors to $Z$ errors, which are analogously detected by the $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kod torik adalah kod CSS, kerana semua penjana penstabilan kita sama ada penjana penstabilan $Z$ atau $X$.\n"
"Ini bermakna ralat $X$ dan ralat $Z$ boleh dikesan (dan mungkin diperbetulkan) secara berasingan.\n"
"Sebenarnya, terdapat simetri yang mudah antara penjana penstabilan $Z$ dan $X$ yang membolehkan kita menganalisis ralat $X$ dan ralat $Z$ dengan cara yang pada dasarnya sama.\n"
"Oleh itu, kita akan fokus pada ralat $X$, yang mungkin dikesan oleh penjana penstabilan $Z$ — tetapi keseluruhan perbincangan berikut boleh diterjemahkan daripada ralat $X$ kepada ralat $Z$, yang secara analoginya dikesan oleh penjana penstabilan $X$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following diagram depicts the effect of an $X$ error on a single qubit.\n"
"Here, the assumption is that our $2L^2$ qubits were previously in a state contained in the code space of the toric code, causing all of the stabilizer generator measurements to output $+1$.\n"
"The $Z$ stabilizer generators detect $X$ errors, and there is one such stabilizer generator for each tile in the figure, so we can indicate the measurement outcome of the corresponding stabilizer generator with the color of that tile:\n"
"$+1$ outcomes are indicated by white tiles and $-1$ outcomes are indicated by gray tiles.\n"
"If a bit-flip error occurs on one of the qubits, the effect is that the stabilizer generator measurements corresponding to the two tiles touching the affected qubit now output $-1$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Gambar rajah berikut menggambarkan kesan ralat $X$ pada satu qubit.\n"
"Di sini, andaiannya ialah $2L^2$ qubit kita sebelumnya berada dalam keadaan yang terkandung dalam ruang kod torik, menyebabkan semua pengukuran penjana penstabilan mengeluarkan $+1.$\n"
"Penjana penstabilan $Z$ mengesan ralat $X$, dan terdapat satu penjana penstabilan sedemikian untuk setiap jubin dalam rajah, jadi kita boleh menunjukkan keputusan pengukuran penjana penstabilan yang sepadan dengan warna jubin itu:\n"
"keputusan $+1$ ditunjukkan dengan jubin putih dan keputusan $-1$ ditunjukkan dengan jubin kelabu.\n"
"Jika ralat balik-bit berlaku pada salah satu qubit, kesannya ialah pengukuran penjana penstabilan yang sepadan dengan dua jubin yang menyentuh qubit yang terjejas kini mengeluarkan $-1.$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![The effect of a single bit-flip error on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-bit-flip.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Kesan ralat balik-bit tunggal pada kod torik](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-bit-flip.svg)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is intuitive when we consider $Z$ stabilizer generators and how they behave.\n"
"In essence, each $Z$ stabilizer generator measures the *parity* of the four qubits that touch the corresponding tile (with respect to the standard basis).\n"
"So, a $+1$ outcome doesn't indicate that no $X$ errors have occurred on these four qubits, but rather it indicates that an *even* number of $X$ errors have occurred on these qubits, whereas a $-1$ outcome indicates that an *odd* number of $X$ errors have occurred.\n"
"A single $X$ error therefore flips the parity of the four bits on both of the tiles it touches, causing the stabilizer generator measurements to output $-1$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ini adalah intuitif apabila kita mempertimbangkan penjana penstabilan $Z$ dan cara ia berkelakuan.\n"
"Pada asasnya, setiap penjana penstabilan $Z$ mengukur *pariti* empat qubit yang menyentuh jubin yang sepadan (berkenaan dengan asas piawai).\n"
"Jadi, keputusan $+1$ tidak menunjukkan bahawa tiada ralat $X$ telah berlaku pada empat qubit ini, tetapi sebaliknya menunjukkan bahawa bilangan *genap* ralat $X$ telah berlaku pada qubit ini, manakala keputusan $-1$ menunjukkan bahawa bilangan *ganjil* ralat $X$ telah berlaku.\n"
"Satu ralat $X$ oleh itu membalikkan pariti empat bit pada kedua-dua jubin yang disentuhnya, menyebabkan pengukuran penjana penstabilan mengeluarkan $-1$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next let's introduce multiple $X$ errors to see what happens.\n"
"In particular, we'll consider a chain of adjacent $X$ errors, where two $X$ errors are adjacent if they affect qubits touching the same tile.\n"
msgstr ""
"Seterusnya mari kita perkenalkan berbilang ralat $X$ untuk melihat apa yang berlaku.\n"
"Khususnya, kita akan mempertimbangkan rantaian ralat $X$ bersebelahan, di mana dua ralat $X$ adalah bersebelahan jika ia menjejaskan qubit yang menyentuh jubin yang sama.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![The effect of a chain of bit-flip errors on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-chain.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Kesan rantaian ralat balik-bit pada kod torik](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-chain.svg)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two $Z$ stabilizer generators at the endpoints of the chain both give the outcome $-1$ in this case, because an odd number of $X$ errors have occurred on those two corresponding tiles.\n"
"All of the other $Z$ stabilizer generators, on the other hand, give the outcome $+1$, including the ones touching the chain but not at the endpoints, because an even number of $X$ errors have occurred on the qubits touching the corresponding tiles.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dua penjana penstabilan $Z$ di hujung rantaian kedua-duanya memberikan keputusan $-1$ dalam kes ini, kerana bilangan ganjil ralat $X$ telah berlaku pada dua jubin yang sepadan tersebut.\n"
"Semua penjana penstabilan $Z$ yang lain pula memberikan keputusan $+1$, termasuk yang menyentuh rantaian tetapi bukan di hujungnya, kerana bilangan genap ralat $X$ telah berlaku pada qubit yang menyentuh jubin yang sepadan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Thus, as long as we have a chain of $X$ errors that has endpoints, the toric code will detect that errors have occurred, resulting in $-1$ measurement outcomes for the $Z$ stabilizer generators corresponding to the endpoints of the chain.\n"
"Note that the actual chain of errors is not revealed, only the endpoints!\n"
"This is OK — in the next subsection we'll see that we don't need to know exactly which qubits were affected by $X$ errors to correct them.\n"
"(The toric code is an example of a highly *degenerate* code, in the sense that it generally does not uniquely identify the errors it corrects.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Oleh itu, selagi kita mempunyai rantaian ralat $X$ yang mempunyai hujung, kod torik akan mengesan bahawa ralat telah berlaku, menghasilkan keputusan pengukuran $-1$ untuk penjana penstabilan $Z$ yang sepadan dengan hujung rantaian.\n"
"Perhatikan bahawa rantaian ralat yang sebenar tidak didedahkan, hanya hujung-hujungnya!\n"
"Ini tiada masalah — dalam subseksyen berikutnya kita akan melihat bahawa kita tidak perlu tahu dengan tepat qubit mana yang terjejas oleh ralat $X$ untuk membetulkannya.\n"
"(Kod torik adalah contoh kod yang sangat *terdegenerasi*, dalam erti kata bahawa ia umumnya tidak mengenal pasti secara unik ralat yang diperbetulkannya.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is, however, possible for a chain of adjacent $X$ errors not to have endpoints, which is to say that a chain of errors could form a *closed loop*, like in the following figure.\n"
msgstr "Namun, adalah mungkin untuk rantaian ralat $X$ bersebelahan tidak mempunyai hujung, iaitu rantaian ralat boleh membentuk *gelung tertutup*, seperti dalam rajah berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A closed loop of bit-flip errors for the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-loop.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Gelung tertutup ralat balik-bit untuk kod torik](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-loop.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In such a case, an even number of $X$ errors have occurred on every tile, so every stabilizer generator measurement results in a $+1$ outcome.\n"
"Closed loops of adjacent $X$ errors are therefore not detected by the code.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalam kes sedemikian, bilangan genap ralat $X$ telah berlaku pada setiap jubin, jadi setiap pengukuran penjana penstabilan menghasilkan keputusan $+1$.\n"
"Gelung tertutup ralat $X$ bersebelahan oleh itu tidak dikesan oleh kod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This might seem disappointing, because we only need four $X$ errors to form a closed loop (and we're hoping for something a lot better than a distance 4 code).\n"
"However, a closed loop of $X$ errors of the form depicted in the previous figure is not actually an error — because it's in the stabilizer!\n"
"Recall that, in addition to the $Z$ stabilizer generators, we also have an $X$ stabilizer generator for each vertex in the lattice.\n"
"And if we multiply adjacent $X$ stabilizer generators together, the result is that we obtain closed loops of $X$ operations.\n"
"For instance, the closed loop in the previous figure can be obtained by multiplying together the $X$ stabilizer generators indicated in the following figure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ini mungkin kelihatan mengecewakan, kerana kita hanya memerlukan empat ralat $X$ untuk membentuk gelung tertutup (dan kita mengharapkan sesuatu yang jauh lebih baik daripada kod jarak 4).\n"
"Namun, gelung tertutup ralat $X$ dalam bentuk seperti yang digambarkan dalam rajah sebelumnya sebenarnya bukan ralat — kerana ia berada dalam penstabilan!\n"
"Ingat bahawa, selain penjana penstabilan $Z$, kita juga mempunyai penjana penstabilan $X$ untuk setiap verteks dalam kekisi.\n"
"Dan jika kita darabkan penjana penstabilan $X$ yang bersebelahan bersama-sama, hasilnya ialah kita mendapat gelung tertutup operasi $X$.\n"
"Sebagai contoh, gelung tertutup dalam rajah sebelumnya boleh diperoleh dengan menggandakan penjana penstabilan $X$ yang ditunjukkan dalam rajah berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A closed loop of bit-flip errors generated by X stabilizer generators](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-loop-generated.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Gelung tertutup ralat balik-bit yang dijana oleh penjana penstabilan X](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-loop-generated.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is, however, not the only type of closed loop of $X$ errors that we can have — and it is not the case that every closed loop of $X$ errors is contained in the stabilizer.\n"
"In particular, the different types of loops can be characterized as follows. Here, a 'horizontal/vertical lattice line' means a full row/column of qubits in the periodic lattice (not the line segments of the loop of qubits affected by the X error).\n"
msgstr ""
"Namun, ini bukan satu-satunya jenis gelung tertutup ralat $X$ yang boleh kita ada — dan bukan semua gelung tertutup ralat $X$ terkandung dalam penstabilan.\n"
"Khususnya, jenis-jenis gelung yang berbeza boleh dicirikan seperti berikut. Di sini, 'garisan kekisi mendatar/menegak' bermaksud baris/lajur penuh qubit dalam kekisi berkala (bukan segmen garisan bagi gelung qubit yang terjejas oleh ralat $X$).\n"

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Closed loops of $X$ errors affecting an even number of qubits on every horizontal lattice line and every vertical lattice line. The example shown above falls into this category, since each lattice line crosses the closed loop of errors once, say crossing into the closed loop, and once crossing back out of the closed loop of errors. Loops of this form are always contained in the stabilizer, as they can effectively be shrunk to the identity by multiplying them by $X$ stabilizer generators.\n"
msgstr "Gelung tertutup ralat $X$ dengan bilangan *genap* ralat $X$ pada setiap garisan mendatar dan setiap garisan menegak qubit. (Contoh yang ditunjukkan di atas tergolong dalam kategori ini, kerana setiap garisan kekisi melintasi gelung tertutup ralat sekali, iaitu memasuki gelung tertutup, dan sekali lagi keluar daripadanya.) Gelung dalam bentuk ini sentiasa terkandung dalam penstabilan, kerana ia boleh dikecutkan menjadi tiada dengan menggandakannya dengan penjana penstabilan $X$.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Closed loops of $X$ errors affecting an odd number of edge qubits on at least one horizontal lattice line or at least one vertical lattice line. Loops of this form are never contained in the stabilizer and therefore represent nontrivial errors that go undetected by the code.\n"
msgstr "Gelung tertutup ralat $X$ dengan bilangan *ganjil* ralat $X$ pada sekurang-kurangnya satu garisan mendatar atau sekurang-kurangnya satu garisan menegak qubit. Gelung dalam bentuk ini tidak pernah terkandung dalam penstabilan dan oleh itu mewakili ralat bukan remeh yang tidak dikesan oleh kod.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "An example of a closed loop of $X$ errors in the second category is shown in the following diagram.\n"
msgstr "Contoh gelung tertutup ralat $X$ dalam kategori kedua ditunjukkan dalam rajah berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A closed loop of bit-flip errors not in the stabilizer](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-logical.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Gelung tertutup ralat balik-bit yang tidak berada dalam penstabilan](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-logical.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Such a loop of $X$ errors is not contained in the stabilizer because every $X$ stabilizer generator places an even number of $X$ operations on each horizontal lattice line and each vertical lattice line of qubits.\n"
"This is therefore an actual example of a nontrivial error that the code fails to detect.\n"
msgstr ""
"Rantaian ralat sedemikian tidak terkandung dalam penstabilan kerana setiap penjana penstabilan $X$ meletakkan bilangan genap operasi $X$ pada setiap garisan mendatar dan setiap garisan menegak qubit.\n"
"Ini oleh itu adalah contoh sebenar ralat bukan remeh yang gagal dikesan oleh kod.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The key is that the only way to form a loop of the second sort is to go around the torus, meaning either around the hole in the middle of the torus, through it, or both.\n"
"Intuitively speaking, a chain of $X$ errors like this cannot be shrunk down to nothing by multiplying it by $X$ stabilizer generators because the topology of a torus prohibits it.\n"
"The toric code is sometimes categorized as a *topological* quantum error correcting code for this reason.\n"
"The shortest that such a loop can be is $L$, and therefore this is the distance of the toric code: any closed loop of $X$ errors with length less than $L$ must fall into the first category, and is therefore contained in the stabilizer; and any chain of $X$ errors with endpoints is detected by the code.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kuncinya ialah satu-satunya cara untuk membentuk gelung jenis kedua ialah mengelilingi torus, sama ada mengelilingi lubang di tengah torus, melaluinya, atau kedua-duanya.\n"
"Secara intuitif, rantaian ralat $X$ seperti ini tidak boleh dikecutkan menjadi tiada dengan menggandakannya dengan penjana penstabilan $X$ kerana topologi torus menghalangnya.\n"
"Kod torik kadang-kadang dikategorikan sebagai kod pembetulan ralat kuantum *topologi* atas sebab ini.\n"
"Panjang minimum gelung sedemikian ialah $L$, dan oleh itu inilah jarak kod torik: mana-mana gelung tertutup ralat $X$ dengan panjang kurang daripada $L$ mesti tergolong dalam kategori pertama, dan oleh itu terkandung dalam penstabilan; dan mana-mana rantaian ralat $X$ dengan hujung dikesan oleh kod.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Given that the toric code uses $2L^2$ qubits to encode $2$ qubits and has distance $L$, it follows that it's a $[[2L^2,2,L]]$ stabilizer code.\n"
msgstr "Memandangkan kod torik menggunakan $2L^2$ qubit untuk mengekod $2$ qubit dan mempunyai jarak $L$, maka ia adalah kod penstabilan $[[2L^2,2,L]]$.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting errors {#correcting-errors}"
msgstr "Membetulkan ralat {#correcting-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We've discussed error *detection* for the toric code, and now we'll briefly discuss how to *correct* errors.\n"
"The toric code is a CSS code, so $X$ errors and $Z$ errors can be detected and corrected independently.\n"
"Keeping our focus on $Z$ stabilizer generators, which detect $X$ errors, let us consider how a chain of $X$ errors can be corrected.\n"
"($Z$ errors are corrected in a symmetric way.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Kita telah membincangkan *pengesanan* ralat untuk kod torik, dan kini kita akan membincangkan secara ringkas cara *membetulkan* ralat.\n"
"Kod torik adalah kod CSS, jadi ralat $X$ dan ralat $Z$ boleh dikesan dan diperbetulkan secara bebas.\n"
"Dengan terus fokus pada penjana penstabilan $Z$, yang mengesan ralat $X$, mari kita pertimbangkan bagaimana rantaian ralat $X$ boleh diperbetulkan.\n"
"(Ralat $Z$ diperbetulkan dengan cara yang simetri.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If a syndrome different from the $(+1,\\ldots,+1)$ syndrome appears when the $Z$ stabilizer generators are measured, the $-1$ outcomes reveal the endpoints of one or more chains of $X$ errors.\n"
"We can attempt to correct these errors by pairing together the $-1$ outcomes and forming a chain of $X$ corrections between them.\n"
"When doing this, it makes sense to choose *shortest paths* along which the corrections take place.\n"
msgstr ""
"Jika sindrom yang berbeza daripada sindrom $(+1,\\ldots,+1)$ muncul apabila penjana penstabilan $Z$ diukur, keputusan $-1$ mendedahkan hujung satu atau lebih rantaian ralat $X$.\n"
"Kita boleh cuba membetulkan ralat ini dengan memasangkan keputusan $-1$ dan membentuk rantaian pembetulan $X$ di antara mereka.\n"
"Apabila melakukan ini, masuk akal untuk memilih *laluan terpendek* di mana pembetulan berlaku.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For instance, consider the following diagram, which depicts a syndrome with two $-1$ outcomes, indicated by gray tiles, caused by a chain of $X$ errors illustrated by the magenta line and circles.\n"
"As we have already remarked, the chain itself is not revealed by the syndrome; only the endpoints are visible.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sebagai contoh, pertimbangkan rajah berikut, yang menggambarkan sindrom dengan dua keputusan $-1$, ditunjukkan oleh jubin kelabu, yang disebabkan oleh rantaian ralat $X$ yang digambarkan oleh garisan dan bulatan magenta.\n"
"Seperti yang telah kita nyatakan, rantaian itu sendiri tidak didedahkan oleh sindrom; hanya hujungnya yang kelihatan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Correcting for X errors with a shortest path](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Membetulkan ralat X dengan laluan terpendek](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To attempt to correct this chain of errors, a shortest path between the $-1$ measurement outcomes is selected and $X$ gates are applied as corrections to the qubits along this path (indicated in yellow in the figure).\n"
"While the corrections may not match up with the actual chain of errors, the errors and corrections together form a closed loop of $X$ operations that is contained in the stabilizer of the code.\n"
"The correction is therefore successful in this situation, as the combined effect of the errors and corrections is to do nothing to an encoded state.\n"
msgstr ""
"Untuk mencuba membetulkan rantaian ralat ini, laluan terpendek antara keputusan pengukuran $-1$ dipilih dan get $X$ digunakan sebagai pembetulan pada qubit sepanjang laluan ini (ditunjukkan dengan warna kuning dalam rajah).\n"
"Walaupun pembetulan mungkin tidak sepadan dengan rantaian ralat yang sebenar, ralat dan pembetulan bersama-sama membentuk gelung tertutup operasi $X$ yang terkandung dalam penstabilan kod.\n"
"Oleh itu pembetulan berjaya dalam situasi ini, kerana kesan gabungan ralat dan pembetulan adalah tidak melakukan apa-apa pada keadaan yang dikodkan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This strategy won't always be successful.\n"
"For example, a different explanation for the same syndrome as in the previous figure is shown in the following figure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Strategi ini tidak akan selalu berjaya.\n"
"Sebagai contoh, penjelasan yang berbeza untuk sindrom yang sama seperti dalam rajah sebelumnya ditunjukkan dalam rajah berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Completing a logical error with corrections](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/correction-complete-error.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Melengkapkan ralat logik dengan pembetulan](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/correction-complete-error.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This time, the same chain of corrections as before fails to correct for this chain of errors, because the combined effect of the errors and corrections is that we obtain a closed loop of $X$ operations that wraps around the torus, and therefore has a nontrivial effect on the code space.\n"
"So, there's no guarantee that the strategy just described, of choosing a shortest path of $X$ corrections between two $-1$ syndrome measurement outcomes, will properly correct the error that caused this syndrome.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kali ini, rantaian pembetulan yang sama seperti sebelumnya gagal untuk membetulkan rantaian ralat ini, kerana kesan gabungan ralat dan pembetulan ialah kita mendapat gelung tertutup operasi $X$ yang mengelilingi torus, dan oleh itu mempunyai kesan bukan remeh pada ruang kod.\n"
"Jadi, tiada jaminan bahawa strategi yang baru dihuraikan, iaitu memilih laluan terpendek pembetulan $X$ antara dua keputusan pengukuran sindrom $-1$, akan membetulkan ralat yang menyebabkan sindrom ini dengan betul.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Perhaps more likely, depending on the noise model, is that a syndrome with more than two $-1$ entries is measured, like the following figure suggests.\n"
msgstr "Mungkin lebih kerap berlaku, bergantung pada model hingar, ialah sindrom dengan lebih daripada dua entri $-1$ diukur, seperti yang dicadangkan oleh rajah berikut.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Multiple correction chains](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/multiple-correction-chains.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Berbilang rantaian pembetulan](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/multiple-correction-chains.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In such a case, there are different correction strategies known.\n"
"One natural strategy is to attempt to pair up the $-1$ measurement outcomes and perform corrections along shortest paths that connect the pairs, as is indicated in the figure in yellow.\n"
"In particular, a *minimum-weight perfect matching* between the $-1$ measurement outcomes can be computed, and then the pairs are connected by shortest paths of $X$ corrections.\n"
"The computation of a minimum-weight perfect matching can be done efficiently with a classical algorithm known as the *blossom algorithm*, which was discovered by Edmonds in the 1960s.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalam kes sedemikian, terdapat strategi pembetulan yang berbeza yang diketahui.\n"
"Satu strategi semula jadi ialah cuba memasangkan keputusan pengukuran $-1$ dan melakukan pembetulan sepanjang laluan terpendek yang menghubungkan pasangan, seperti yang ditunjukkan dalam rajah dengan warna kuning.\n"
"Khususnya, *padanan sempurna berwajaran minimum* antara keputusan pengukuran $-1$ boleh dikira, kemudian pasangan dihubungkan oleh laluan terpendek pembetulan $X$.\n"
"Pengiraan padanan sempurna berwajaran minimum boleh dilakukan dengan cekap menggunakan algoritma klasik yang dikenali sebagai *algoritma bunga*, yang ditemui oleh Edmonds pada tahun 1960-an.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This approach is generally not optimal for the most typically studied noise models, but based on numerical simulations it works very well in practice below a noise rate of approximately 10\\%, assuming independent Pauli errors where $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, are equally likely.\n"
"Increasing $L$ doesn't have a significant effect on the break-even point at which the code starts to help, but does lead to a faster decrease in the probability for a logical error as the error rate decreases past the break-even point.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pendekatan ini umumnya tidak optimum untuk model hingar yang paling biasa dikaji, tetapi berdasarkan simulasi berangka ia berfungsi dengan sangat baik dalam praktik di bawah kadar hingar kira-kira 10\\%, dengan mengandaikan ralat Pauli bebas di mana $X,$ $Y,$ dan $Z,$ adalah sama berkemungkinan.\n"
"Meningkatkan $L$ tidak memberi kesan ketara pada titik impas di mana kod mula membantu, tetapi mengakibatkan penurunan yang lebih pantas dalam kebarangkalian ralat logik apabila kadar ralat melepasi titik impas.\n"
